  "passages": [
    {
      "passage_uid": "PU-dd5305fcbda2e5e1",
      "source_id": "S-2c95d65f4117",
      "char_start": 12888,
      "char_end": 13436,
      "text": "GDANSK Image With their intensely Gen Z branding and celebrity collabs, prebiotic soda brands like Olipop and Poppi successfully introduced fibre into the Hot Girl lexicon by giving it a pastel makeover and citrus flavouring. The wellness-adjacent drinks, sold as a gut-friendly alternative to more sugary sodas, rocketed in popularity, leading to PepsiCo acquiring Poppi for $2bn in March 2025. Far from being another TikTok diet fad, we believe there’s a significant, longer-term change happening here. Big macro-level shifts tend to be reactive.",
      "text_sha256": "357fe58dda083282732fbc4b0656a2fb12edbab46c4bcc13cd0707368abcb904",
      "identity_decision": "ok",
      "identity_reason": "official-domain document",
      "statement_type": "historical_activity",
      "flags": [],
      "dates_mentioned": [
        "March 2025"
      ],
      "relevance_terms": [
        "brand",
        "collab",
        "tiktok"
      ],
      "score": 19,
      "official_source": true,
      "selection_status": "selected",
      "rejection_reasons": [],
      "packet_id": "P1",
      "source_url": "https://www.oatly.com/en-us/things-we-do/initiatives/future-of-taste",
      "document_identity": "official_domain",
      "publication_date": null
    },
    {
      "passage_uid": "PU-e7b3e6ff00664698",
      "source_id": "S-2c95d65f4117",
      "char_start": 3243,
      "char_end": 3824,
      "text": "trends setting the pace. Matchamania has opened the floodgates for a broader shift in global tastes, especially in the West. We’re already seeing a myriad of specialist East and Southeast Asian ingredients appearing on menus worldwide. From calamansi to ube, pandan to osmanthus, the list of fruits, vegetables and herbs discussed in joyful tones by the baristas of Europe and North America is long and bursting with colour. Image Take ube, a sweet purple yam native to the Philippines whose popularity is gathering pace around the world, from lattes in LA to ice cream in Iceland.",
      "text_sha256": "b8e3ed85234d19caa1a2609ba711d3ae9031b1e503b7e4f9b8a006bf9e6d47c3",
      "identity_decision": "ok",
      "identity_reason": "official-domain document",
      "statement_type": "actual_activity",
      "flags": [],
      "dates_mentioned": [],
      "relevance_terms": [],
      "score": 14,
      "official_source": true,
      "selection_status": "selected",
      "rejection_reasons": [],
      "packet_id": "P2",
      "source_url": "https://www.oatly.com/en-us/things-we-do/initiatives/future-of-taste",
      "document_identity": "official_domain",
      "publication_date": null
    },
    {
      "passage_uid": "PU-a3e8d6345bd2637c",
      "source_id": "S-2c95d65f4117",
      "char_start": 3825,
      "char_end": 4505,
      "text": "Pandan, a fragrant green plant, is also starting to make loud noises on London’s food and drink circuit, as one cafe owner discovered: “When we first opened, we had to spend, like, a whole year trying to explain what pandan is. And now when I go into some random cafes, I see pandan this, pandan that!” Aromatic yuzu emerged throughout the research as a key driver of a possible citrus renaissance, used particularly in homemade syrups, with calamansi also mentioned in Australia. “I see taro or ube coming up or calamansi and yuzu. So, I feel like there's some movement of East Asian and Southeast Asian produce into the mainstream.” LA Nostalgia plays a role in this trend, too.",
      "text_sha256": "6584bcd27e0cf8b9ad93b0356881a770967fba09cee291ad8dd52c0891dddf2d",
      "identity_decision": "ok",
      "identity_reason": "official-domain document",
      "statement_type": "actual_activity",
      "flags": [
        "forward_looking_statement"
      ],
      "dates_mentioned": [],
      "relevance_terms": [],
      "score": 14,
      "official_source": true,
      "selection_status": "selected",
      "rejection_reasons": [],
      "packet_id": "P3",
      "source_url": "https://www.oatly.com/en-us/things-we-do/initiatives/future-of-taste",
      "document_identity": "official_domain",
      "publication_date": null
    },
    {
      "passage_uid": "PU-eaa8649ae262c5ab",
      "source_id": "S-560384889eb5",
      "char_start": 1916,
      "char_end": 2535,
      "text": "When today's CEO Toni Petersson drank Oatly's oat milk for the first time, he was impressed by the sustainability of the production and the low sugar content. However, he says that he found drinking oats “unpleasant” and that he wasn't particularly inspired by Oatly's accompanying marketing. But he saw potential. After all, soy and almond milk was becoming increasingly popular. So he decided to take over the company and target the Oatly brand at younger, trendier milk drinkers. Today, the company has found an audience among hipsters and coffee fans who appreciate the taste and sustainable production of oat milk.",
      "text_sha256": "84180aa0d618d719ad0451d5f5df31cbf9fa461f48bd18e981274b842d6f4a5b",
      "identity_decision": "ok",
      "identity_reason": "third-party passage names the company in a confirmed document",
      "statement_type": "descriptive",
      "flags": [],
